// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`--help snapshots > agent 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite agent [options] [command]

Install TestSprite guidance into coding-agent config (Claude Code, Cursor,
Cline, Antigravity, Kiro, Windsurf, Copilot, Codex)

Options:
  -h, --help                      display help for command

Commands:
  install [options] [targets...]  Write the TestSprite agent skills into a
                                  project
  list                            List installable agent targets and skills
  status [options]                Check installed skill files against this CLI
                                  version
  help [command]                  display help for command
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > agent install 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite agent install [options] [targets...]

Write the TestSprite agent skills (verification loop + first-run onboarding)
into a project for a coding agent. Targets may be positional (\`agent install
cursor codex\`) and/or via --target; they are merged.

Options:
  --target <t>    Agent target(s): claude, cursor, cline, antigravity, kiro,
                  windsurf, copilot, codex (comma-separated or repeated).
                  Merged with any positional target(s). (default: [])
  --skill <name>  Skill(s) to install: testsprite-verify, testsprite-onboard,
                  testsprite-ci (comma-separated or repeated; default: all)
                  (default: [])
  --dir <path>    Project root to write into (default: cwd)
  --force         For own-file targets: overwrite existing file (a .bak backup
                  is kept). For codex (managed-section): replaces the section
                  unconditionally; user content outside the section is never
                  destroyed.
  -h, --help      display help for command

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > agent list 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite agent list [options]

List the agent targets and skills this CLI can install and where each lands
(run \`testsprite agent status\` to see what is installed here)

Options:
  -h, --help  display help for command

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > apply 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite apply [options]

Apply what the agent proposed, as the portal card does: save the plan, the
environment, the schedule or the project's settings, or start the run. A run it
starts is waited for, and its results go back to the agent, which reads them
and replies.

Options:
  --only <tests>       apply only these tests of the plan, by the refs it lists
                       (comma-separated)
  --exclude <tests>    apply the plan without these tests (comma-separated)
  --secret-stdin       for a card that asks for a secret (a password, a token):
                       read it from what is piped in
  --answer <n=choice>  for a card that asks questions: answer question n with
                       an option's letter (letters comma-separated when it
                       takes several) or your own words (repeatable)
  --env <name>         for a card that starts a run: run it on this environment
                       instead of the one the card names
  --card <id>          the card to decide, by the id the conversation showed
                       (default: the one last shown in this folder); one
                       decided meanwhile is refused
  --project <id>       project id (default: TESTSPRITE_PROJECT_ID, else the one
                       last used in this folder)
  --conversation <id>  conversation id (default: the one last started from this
                       folder)
  --no-wait            return as soon as the agent has it, without following it
  --timeout <seconds>  stop following after this long (default: no limit in a
                       terminal, 540s otherwise); the agent keeps working
  --steps              show each proposed test step by step
  -h, --help           display help for command

Examples:
  testsprite apply
  testsprite apply --exclude 3,CHK-009
  testsprite apply --answer 1=b --answer 2=a,c
  testsprite apply --env staging

Exit codes:
  0   the agent proposed something or replied
  1   the agent stopped before it finished, or the conversation ended
  5   invalid input (no project, a missing file, --diff outside a repository)
  6   the conversation is not waiting for this (e.g. apply with nothing proposed)
  7   still working at --timeout; follow it with \`testsprite status --wait\`
  13  the agent is not enabled for this account

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > auth 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite auth [options] [command]

Manage TestSprite credentials

Options:
  -h, --help      display help for command

Commands:
  status          Show the active profile and its credentials
  remove          Remove the stored API key for the active profile
  help [command]  display help for command

To add credentials, run: testsprite setup
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > ci 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite ci [options] [command]

Run tests in CI and scaffold CI integration

Options:
  -h, --help                 display help for command

Commands:
  run [options]              Run project tests, wait for results, and apply the
                             CI gate
  init [options] <platform>  Write a CI workflow that runs your test cases on
                             pull requests (github)
  help [command]             display help for command
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > ci init 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite ci init [options] <platform>

Write a CI workflow (platform: github) that runs the project's TestSprite test
cases against each
pull request's preview deployment; each run spends credits. Replace the
"REPLACE ME" step in the
generated file with your preview URL. Needs the TESTSPRITE_API_KEY repo secret
(see --set-secret).

Options:
  --project <id>       project id to run (auto-detected if you have exactly
                       one)
  --env <name>         environment whose sign-in each PR run uses (default: the
                       project's default environment, written out by name)
  --timeout <s>        max seconds ci run waits for a terminal verdict (1-3600;
                       omitted: ci run's default)
  --path <file>        workflow file to write, under .github/workflows/ (.yml
                       or .yaml) (default: ".github/workflows/testsprite.yml")
  --force              overwrite an existing workflow with different content
                       (keeps a .bak) (default: false)
  --set-secret         set the TESTSPRITE_API_KEY repo secret via gh (if
                       installed + authenticated); OVERWRITES an existing
                       secret of that name (default: false)
  --repo <owner/name>  target repo for --set-secret (gh infers from the cwd if
                       omitted)
  -h, --help           display help for command

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > ci run 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite ci run [options]

Run project tests, wait for results, and apply the CI gate

Options:
  --project <id>                   project id (defaults to
                                   TESTSPRITE_PROJECT_ID)
  --all                            run all project tests (already the default)
  --filter <substr>                only run tests whose name contains this
                                   non-empty substring (case-insensitive); omit
                                   to run every test
  --env <name>                     the environment whose sign-in the run uses
                                   (unknown names fail). In a pull request,
                                   with --target-url, the run happens in a
                                   temporary clone of it at the preview
                                   address, named github-pr-<N> like a GitHub
                                   App run
  --target-url <url>               frontend tests: the address to test,
                                   typically this pull request's preview
                                   deployment
  --target-url-template <pattern>  the preview address as a pattern filled from
                                   the pull request: {pr}, {branch},
                                   {branch-slug}, {sha}, {short-sha} (e.g.
                                   https://app-git-{branch-slug}-team.vercel.app);
                                   instead of --target-url
  --wait-for-status <name>         wait for this commit status context or check
                                   run on the head commit to succeed before
                                   running (the name as the pull request shows
                                   it, e.g. "Vercel"); a failed or skipped
                                   build exits 0 with a notice
  --status-timeout <s>             max seconds --wait-for-status waits (1-3600;
                                   default 900)
  --skip-preflight                 skip the pre-charge reachability probe for
                                   --target-url (default: false)
  --timeout <s>                    max seconds to wait for results (1-3600)
                                   (default: "600")
  --execution-report-timeout <s>   max seconds to wait for the execution report
                                   that fills the job summary (1-3600)
                                   (default: "60")
  --no-execution-report            keep the job summary to the local results
                                   table; read and write no execution report
  --max-concurrency <n>            maximum concurrent polls (1-100) (default:
                                   "50")
  --idempotency-key <key>          idempotency key for the batch dispatch
  --fail-on-skipped                fail if any requested test was not run
                                   (default: warn)
  --allow-empty                    allow zero dispatched tests (default: exit
                                   5)
  --gh-output                      force GitHub annotations and job summary
                                   outside Actions
  --summary-file <path>            write the unified CI summary JSON to this
                                   file
  --report <format>                also write a report (junit); disabled by
                                   default
  --report-file <path>             JUnit report destination (requires --report
                                   junit)
  --report-suite-name <name>       JUnit suite name (default:
                                   testsprite:<projectId>)
  -h, --help                       display help for command

Always waits for results. Failed tests and timeouts fail the gate.
Skipped tests warn unless --fail-on-skipped; missing test ids and unknown
environments always fail. Every selected test is seeded into ONE run
and the server paces dispatch, as for a GitHub App run.
In GitHub Actions the pull request is read from the event (a deployment_status
event is looked up by commit with GITHUB_TOKEN); draft PRs and deployments
with no pull request are skipped with a notice and zero step outputs. The run
is stamped with the PR, so the Portal links back to it. ci run does not read
the deployment's state or environment: filter deployment_status events with
the job's \`if:\` (state == 'success', not production), or a failed or
production deployment is gated too.
Writes total, passed, failed (includes blocked), blocked, failedOnly, skipped and timedOut to GITHUB_OUTPUT.
On a supporting backend the job summary is the execution report — the same content
the GitHub App posts as its PR comment — and execution-report-file /
execution-report-markdown-file name its JSON and Markdown for later steps.
Reports and outputs are emitted before the exit code is decided.
When the platform posts only a commit status or check (no deployment event),
--wait-for-status <name> holds the run until it succeeds and --target-url-template
derives the preview address from the pull request; a pattern that cannot be filled
is refused, never guessed. In a pull request the run happens in a github-pr-<N> clone
of --env at --target-url, so backend tests whose code uses VAR_{url} follow the preview
too; outside one, with --env, they use that environment's own URL. PR comments are not
yet supported.


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > discard 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite discard [options]

Stop the agent, turn down a card waiting on you, and put back what the
conversation changed in the project and never saved, so other conversations are
not held by it. Nothing saved to the suite is touched. The conversation stays
readable, and reads as discarded until a reply picks it up again.

Options:
  --project <id>       project id (default: TESTSPRITE_PROJECT_ID, else the one
                       last used in this folder)
  --conversation <id>  conversation id (default: the one last started from this
                       folder)
  -h, --help           display help for command

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > plan 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite plan [options] [request...]

Start a conversation with the project's agent, the same one the portal has. It
explores your app, writes or fixes test cases, and proposes them as a plan you
apply or reject. Say what to test, or what went wrong; attach your change with
--diff and documents with --file.

Arguments:
  request              what to test, in your own words

Options:
  --diff [base]        attach your change since <base> (default: where this
                       branch left the default branch)
  --file <path>        attach a document: a PRD, a ticket, a spec (repeatable)
  --model <id>         the model the agent runs on, like claude-haiku-5-5
                       (default: the portal default)
  --project <id>       project id (default: TESTSPRITE_PROJECT_ID, else the one
                       last used in this folder)
  --no-wait            return as soon as the agent has it, without following it
  --timeout <seconds>  stop following after this long (default: no limit in a
                       terminal, 540s otherwise); the agent keeps working
  --steps              show each proposed test step by step
  -h, --help           display help for command

Examples:
  testsprite plan "cover the new coupon rules" --diff --file docs/coupons-prd.md
  testsprite plan "the checkout tests fail since the redesign; fix the tests, report real bugs"
  testsprite plan --diff --no-wait     # start it and come back with \`testsprite status --wait\`

Exit codes:
  0   the agent proposed something or replied
  1   the agent stopped before it finished, or the conversation ended
  5   invalid input (no project, a missing file, --diff outside a repository)
  6   the conversation is not waiting for this (e.g. apply with nothing proposed)
  7   still working at --timeout; follow it with \`testsprite status --wait\`
  13  the agent is not enabled for this account

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > project 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite project [options] [command]

Manage TestSprite projects

Options:
  -h, --help                     display help for command

Commands:
  list [options]                 List projects visible to the API key
  get <project-id>               Get a project by id
  create [options]               Create a project; add where it runs with
                                 \`project env create\`
  update [options] <project-id>  Update a project's name, extra instructions or
                                 test-id attributes
  delete [options] <project-id>  Permanently delete a project (requires
                                 --confirm)
  env                            Manage a project's environments
  help [command]                 display help for command
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > project create 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite project create [options]

Create a project; add where it runs with \`project env create\`

Options:
  --type <frontend|backend>    project type (required)
  --name <name>                project name (required)
  --instruction <text>         extra instructions: facts and rules the agent
                               keeps in mind when it explores the app, writes
                               tests or runs them
  --test-id-attributes <list>  comma-separated DOM attributes the engine should
                               prefer as locators, highest priority first (e.g.
                               data-element,data-testid). Unique values are
                               exported as page.locator('[attr="…"]').
  --idempotency-key <token>    opaque idempotency token. Defaults to a UUIDv4
                               minted per invocation.
  -h, --help                   display help for command

Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > project env create 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite project env create [options] <project-id>

Create an environment (--name and one of --url / --local are required).
Exit codes:
  0  success
  3  auth error
  4  project not found
  5  validation error
  6  conflict (name already exists) / precondition (OTP or SSO on a local environment)
  7  unsupported (OTP/SSO in BYOC)
  10 service unavailable
  11 rate limited
  13 SSO feature gated
  14 client too old

Options:
  --name <name>                  environment name, unique within the project
                                 (required)
  --url <url>                    address runs open (public http/https, or a
                                 loopback http origin with an explicit port)
  --local <port>                 shorthand for --url http://localhost:<port>
                                 (1-65535; excludes --url; frontend only)
  --skip-preflight               skip the local TCP listener check before
                                 creating
  --username <user>              test-account username the browser logs in with
  --password <pw>                test-account password (prefer --password-file,
                                 exclusive)
  --password-file <path>         read the password from a file instead of the
                                 command line
  --var <KEY=VALUE>              set a custom variable (repeatable; max 50,
                                 4096 bytes each, key [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*,
                                 reserved names like url/username/password
                                 rejected); shown in plain text by \`env get\`,
                                 not for secrets (default: [])
  --sign-in <mode>               public, account, otp, or manual (aliases:
                                 none, credentials, sso). manual: log in once
                                 in the Portal, runs reuse that session (not
                                 signed in until then). otp and manual: not for
                                 local environments
  --otp-channel <email,sms>      OTP channels: email and/or sms; repeat or
                                 comma-separate (default: email)
  --session-ttl <seconds|never>  SSO session reuse lifetime: 1–2592000 seconds
                                 or never (manual only)
  --auth <type>                  backend projects: how runs sign in to the API
                                 — none, bearer, basic or apikey; replaces a
                                 sign-in
  --credential <value>           backend projects: static credential value
                                 (prefer --credential-file, exclusive)
  --credential-file <path>       backend projects: read the static credential
                                 from a file
  --header <"Name: value">       backend projects: an extra request header
                                 (repeatable; replaces the stored set)
  --header-file <path>           backend projects: extra request headers, one
                                 "Name: value" per line (for secret values)
  --auto-auth-file <path>        backend projects: JSON sign-in that refreshes
                                 the credential; replaces a static one (see
                                 below)
  --set-default                  make this the project's default environment
                                 (default: false)
  --idempotency-key <token>      opaque idempotency token. Defaults to a UUIDv4
                                 minted per invocation.
  -h, --help                     display help for command

API sign-in (backend projects): what runs sign in to the API with, kept on the environment as the
project's agent reads it. --auth none|bearer|basic|apikey with --credential-file; --header "Name: value"
(repeatable) and --header-file (one "Name: value" per line, for secret values) replace the extra
headers every request carries; --auto-auth-file holds a sign-in that refreshes the credential, e.g.
  {"method": "password", "inject": "bearer", "loginUrl": "https://api.example.com/login",
   "loginBodyTemplate": "{\\"email\\":\\"{{username}}\\",\\"password\\":\\"{{password}}\\"}",
   "username": "qa@example.com", "password": "<secret>", "tokenPath": "$.access_token"}
method: password | refresh_token (tokenEndpoint, clientId, refreshToken) | aws_cognito_refresh (region,
clientId, refreshToken); inject: bearer | header | cookie (header and cookie take injectKey). The file is
sent as it is: the server refuses a field it does not know, a secret left out keeps its stored value,
"enabled": false saves it switched off. Saving a sign-in runs a trial login and needs a plan with
advanced environment settings; --auth replaces a sign-in with a static credential. Which endpoint
needs which header is decided in the Portal.
For Basic auth, supply username:password or its base64 encoding.


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

exports[`--help snapshots > project env update 1`] = `
"Usage: testsprite project env update [options] <project-id> <name>

Change an environment's URL, credentials, custom variables, name or (backend projects) API sign-in; a frontend sign-in mode changes in the Portal.
Exit codes:
  0  success
  3  auth error
  4  project (or environment) not found
  5  validation error
  6  conflict (name already exists) / precondition (credentials or --local on an SSO or OTP environment; change its sign-in in the Portal)

Options:
  --url <url>                new address runs open (public http/https, or a
                             loopback http origin with an explicit port)
  --local <port>             shorthand for --url http://localhost:<port>
                             (1-65535; excludes --url)
  --skip-preflight           skip the local TCP listener check before the
                             update
  --username <user>          new test-account username
  --password <pw>            new test-account password (prefer --password-file,
                             exclusive)
  --password-file <path>     read the new password from a file
  --clear-credentials        remove stored username and password; disable
                             account login
  --var <KEY=VALUE>          merge a custom variable (repeatable; max 50, 4096
                             bytes each, key [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*, reserved
                             names like url/username/password rejected); shown
                             in plain text by \`env get\`, not for secrets
                             (default: [])
  --rename <new-name>        rename the environment (runs keep referring to it
                             by the new name)
  --auth <type>              backend projects: how runs sign in to the API —
                             none, bearer, basic or apikey; replaces a sign-in
  --credential <value>       backend projects: static credential value (prefer
                             --credential-file, exclusive)
  --credential-file <path>   backend projects: read the static credential from
                             a file
  --header <"Name: value">   backend projects: an extra request header
                             (repeatable; replaces the stored set)
  --header-file <path>       backend projects: extra request headers, one
                             "Name: value" per line (for secret values)
  --auto-auth-file <path>    backend projects: JSON sign-in that refreshes the
                             credential; replaces a static one (see below)
  --idempotency-key <token>  opaque idempotency token. Defaults to a UUIDv4
                             minted per invocation.
  -h, --help                 display help for command

API sign-in (backend projects): what runs sign in to the API with, kept on the environment as the
project's agent reads it. --auth none|bearer|basic|apikey with --credential-file; --header "Name: value"
(repeatable) and --header-file (one "Name: value" per line, for secret values) replace the extra
headers every request carries; --auto-auth-file holds a sign-in that refreshes the credential, e.g.
  {"method": "password", "inject": "bearer", "loginUrl": "https://api.example.com/login",
   "loginBodyTemplate": "{\\"email\\":\\"{{username}}\\",\\"password\\":\\"{{password}}\\"}",
   "username": "qa@example.com", "password": "<secret>", "tokenPath": "$.access_token"}
method: password | refresh_token (tokenEndpoint, clientId, refreshToken) | aws_cognito_refresh (region,
clientId, refreshToken); inject: bearer | header | cookie (header and cookie take injectKey). The file is
sent as it is: the server refuses a field it does not know, a secret left out keeps its stored value,
"enabled": false saves it switched off. Saving a sign-in runs a trial login and needs a plan with
advanced environment settings; --auth replaces a sign-in with a static credential. Which endpoint
needs which header is decided in the Portal.
For Basic auth, supply username:password or its base64 encoding.


Global options (--dry-run, --output, --profile, --endpoint-url, --request-timeout, --verbose, --debug):
  testsprite --help
"
`;

